    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186175972,
      "id": "87b371d9-6172-5ac0-a105-c9887542f0a7",
      "title": "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "30f9e454-2426-561e-ada6-71205c098e70",
      ":question/problem": "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,?⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3{y}^{2}=z.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is $3{y}^{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6de1edfb-5f8c-5107-a207-25b6615b990b",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
